  /* ── HIDING, LAST AND SPELLED OUT ─────────────────────────────────────────
     This rule was six declarations higher up and read simply `.wk-layer[hidden]
     { display: none }`, and two layers ignored it completely: the model pair and
     the production panel, which set `display: grid` and `display: flex` through
     `.wk-layer.is-pair` and `.wk-layer.is-work`.

     Both of those are class + class, which after Astro appends its scope
     attribute weighs exactly the same as class + attribute — a tie, broken by
     source order, and they came later. The result was a stage with three layers
     drawn on top of each other: the product, the portrait and the checklist all
     at once, at every step. The JavaScript was right the whole time; `hidden`
     was set correctly on every one of them.

     So the rule sits at the very end of the block and names the two selectors
     it has to beat, rather than reaching for !important. Anything added later
     that sets `display` on a layer has to be added here too — which is the
     point: it is a list you cannot forget quietly. */
  .wk-layer[hidden],
  .wk-layer.is-pair[hidden],
  .wk-layer.is-work[hidden],
  .wk-layer.is-out[hidden] { display: none; }

